.TH KILL 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
kill \- send a signal to processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \-s
.I signal
.RB "| " \-\c
.IR signal ]
.IR pid ...
.br
.B kill \-l
.RI [ signal ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
sends a signal, by default
.BR TERM ,
to each process whose ID it is given.
With
.BR \-l ,
it lists signals instead and sends nothing.
Windows has no signals, so they are emulated; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Each
.I pid
is a decimal Windows process ID, as listed by
.BR ps (1).
A positive
.I pid
signals that process only.
Zero, or a negative number
.B \-\c
.IR n ,
signals every descendant of a process and then the process itself:
the process running
.B kill
for 0, and process
.I n
otherwise.
The descendants are the children, grandchildren, and so on, found
through the parent process IDs of the processes running when
.B kill
looks.
If any of them cannot be signaled, the operand fails and is reported
with its own
.IR pid ,
but the others are still signaled.
.PP
An operand may hold several PIDs separated by single spaces.
An empty operand is ignored.
.PP
.B kill
writes nothing on success.
It reports each failure on standard error and goes on to the next
operand:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
kill: can\(aqt kill pid 1234: Invalid argument
kill: invalid number \(aqabc\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SS Signals
A signal is a name or a number.
A name is case-insensitive and may begin with
.BR SIG ,
as in
.BR sigterm .
A number is decimal.
The signals are:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
 0  EXIT    4  ILL     11  SEGV    22  ABRT
 1  HUP     8  FPE     13  PIPE
 2  INT     9  KILL    15  TERM
 3  QUIT
.EE
.RE
.PP
Any other number from 0 to 22 is accepted, but sending it fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
So does an empty name, or
.B SIG
alone, which is taken as 5.
Other names, such as
.BR USR1 ,
.BR ALRM ,
.BR STOP ,
and
.BR CONT ,
and numbers above 22 are errors, and nothing is sent.
Every signal except 0 ends the target process.
They differ only in the exit code the process gets, and in how
.B KILL
is delivered; see
.BR NOTES .
Signal 0 sends nothing, but fails if the process cannot be signaled, so
it tests for a process.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only at the start of the arguments, and cannot be
grouped.
Everything after them is an operand, even if it begins with
.BR \- ,
so a negative
.I pid
may follow a signal option.
.TP
.B \-l
List signals and send nothing.
It must be the first argument.
With no operands, write one line per signal, without
.BR EXIT :
its number right-aligned to width 2, then
.BR ) ,
a space, and its name.
.IP
For each operand, write the name if it is a number, or the number if it
is a name.
A number is first reduced modulo 128, so that an exit status of
.RI "128 + " n
gives the name of signal
.IR n ,
and a result with no name is written as a number.
An operand that is not a signal is reported, and ends the list with
status 1.
.TP
.BI "\-s " signal
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR TERM .
The same as
.BI \- signal\fR.
The signal may also be attached, as in
.BR \-sTERM ,
unless the argument is itself a signal name, such as
.BR \-segv .
.TP
.BI \- signal
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR TERM .
.I signal
is a name or a number, as described in
.BR Signals .
.TP
.B \-\-
As the first argument, end the options and send
.BR TERM ,
so that the first
.I pid
may be negative.
After a signal option it is an operand, which is not a valid PID.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and not by the built-in
.B kill
of the kit's shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every signal was sent, or the list was written.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, such as an unknown signal or no operands, or exactly one
failure.
.TP
.B >1
The number of failures: each operand that is not a valid PID, and each
PID that could not be signaled.
The status is this count modulo 256, so 256 failures give 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a process to end:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill 1234
.EE
.RE
.PP
End a process without letting it clean up:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill \-KILL 1234
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a process exists and can be signaled:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill \-0 1234 && echo running
.EE
.RE
.PP
End a process and all its descendants, using the negative form of its
ID.
The
.B \-\-
is needed only when no signal option precedes the ID:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill \-\- \-1234
$ kill \-KILL \-1234
.EE
.RE
.PP
End a background job of the shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill %1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Name the signal that ended a command, given its exit status:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill \-l 143
TERM
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the signals:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ kill \-l
 1) HUP
 2) INT
 3) QUIT
 4) ILL
 8) FPE
 9) KILL
11) SEGV
13) PIPE
15) TERM
22) ABRT
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B KILL
calls
.BR TerminateProcess ,
which gives the process no chance to clean up.
Any other signal except 0 makes the process call
.B ExitProcess
from a thread that
.B kill
injects into it.
No signal handler runs in either case, and
.B INT
does not send Ctrl+C: it ends the process like
.BR TERM .
.PP
The exit code of a signaled process is the signal number shifted left
24 bits, such as
.B 0x0F000000
for
.BR TERM ,
which the kit's shell reports as 128 plus the signal number, so
.B $?\&
is 143 for a process ended by
.BR TERM .
.PP
A signal is delivered asynchronously.
The command returns once it has created the thread, and does not report
a failure to create it, so the process may exit a moment later, or not
at all.
Injecting a thread works only when
.B kill
and the target are both 32-bit or both 64-bit.
Otherwise every signal except
.BR KILL ,
including 0, fails.
.PP
The reason after
.B can\(aqt kill pid
is
.B Invalid argument
for a process that does not exist or has already exited, or for a
number with no name, and
.B Permission denied
for a process that cannot be opened with the access needed, such as a
system process, or that has the other bitness.
.PP
Windows reuses process IDs, so a process whose parent has exited is
taken for a child of any later process that gets the parent's ID.
If reused IDs make a process its own ancestor, a negative
.I pid
or 0 whose descendants include it makes
.B kill
run without end.
.PP
Differences from the usual Unix command:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no process groups, so a negative
.I pid
selects descendants, and there is no PID
.B \-1
for all processes.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the signals listed above exist, with no job control signals such
as
.B STOP
and
.BR CONT .
.IP \(bu 2
Numbers follow the Windows C runtime:
.B ABRT
is 22, not 6, and 6 cannot be sent.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.B \-n
or
.B \-L
options.
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status counts failures.
.PP
In the kit's shell
.B kill
is a built-in that runs this same code inside the shell's process, so
.B kill 0
ends the shell itself and everything it started.
Its messages begin with the name of the shell instead of
.BR kill: .
It also accepts job specifications such as
.B %1
in place of PIDs, and signals each running process of the job and all
of their descendants.
An unknown job is a shell error with status 2.
.PP
For a command run in the background,
.B $!\&
is the ID of a shell process, which runs the program as its child
unless the program is a busybox applet that runs inside it.
Then
.B kill $!\&
ends only that shell and leaves the program running, but
.B kill %1
and
.B kill \-\- \-$!\&
end both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR killall (1),
.BR pgrep (1),
.BR pidof (1),
.BR pkill (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR sh (1)
